<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zhanjiang</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Fort-Bayard</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zhanjiang is a port city in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangdong">Guangdong</a> province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leizhou-peninsula">Leizhou Peninsula</a>. It grew from the French leased territory of Guangzhouwan, whose seat was named Fort-Bayard; the territory was returned to China in 1945 and the city took its present name. It is now a major port and the headquarters of China's South Sea Fleet.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zhanjiang.jpg" alt="Zhanjiang" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DragonSamYU · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zhanjiang_Bay_Bridge_-_20181030.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Zhanjiang is a prefecture-level city that administers the entire <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leizhou-peninsula">Leizhou Peninsula</a> and lies directly opposite Haikou, the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hainan">Hainan</a>, across the Qiongzhou Strait. It is the southernmost port on the coast of mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and a centre of commerce and navigation for much of south-western China. The city sits on an inlet of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-china-sea">South China Sea</a>, southwest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a>, and its climate is humid subtropical, with heavy rain from April to September.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin dynasty</a> the area belonged to the Xiang Shire administrative division, and under the Han dynasty Xuwen County was set up as the seat for the whole <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leizhou-peninsula">Leizhou Peninsula</a>. The port became one of the earliest departure points on the Maritime Silk Road. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> many Putian (Hinghwa) colonists settled in the region, later forming the ethnolinguistic majority of the peninsula, alongside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baiyue">Baiyue</a>, Cantonese, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanka">Tanka</a> and foreign merchants.</p><p>In 1898 the area was a small fishing port when the French established control and forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> to lease a 99-year concession called Guangzhouwan. Much of the local population had fled, so Cantonese peasants from north of Leizhou were brought in, which made Yue Chinese the dominant local language. The French developed the port as Fort Bayard, intending it as a logistical centre for their concessions in southern China, though poverty and underdevelopment limited the effort. They held the enclave until 1943, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> occupied it during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>. After the war it was briefly returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> before being ceded to China. The Republic of China renamed the area Zhanjiang, a variant of Zhanchuan, the name of an earlier county.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> was established in 1949 the city grew in strategic and economic importance, and by 1957 it had a modern deep-water port able to handle ships of up to 50,000 tons. In 1984, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deng-xiaoping">Deng Xiaoping</a>'s policy of reform and opening-up, it was designated an open city to attract foreign investment. In the early 1990s a rail line linked it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a> and was later extended to Hai'an at the southern tip of the peninsula.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted a resident population of 6,981,236. Leizhou Min is the prestige language in several districts, Yue Chinese is spoken in Potou District and other areas settled in the French period, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hakka-people">Hakka</a> is spoken in Lianjiang County. The local Leizhou Opera has a history of over 300 years. Zhanjiang is the headquarters of the South Sea Fleet of the People's Liberation Army Navy. Its port is a natural deep-water harbour protected by three outer islands, and the old French quarter in Chikan survives as Chikan Old Street, with arched windows, balconies and tiled roofs.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zhanjiang” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The territory was attached to French Indochina, governed by an administrator under the governor-general in Hanoi, with its seat at <b>Fort-Bayard</b> (modern Zhanjiang).</blockquote><a href="/state/guangzhouwan">Guangzhouwan</a></li></ul></section>
